---
/**
 * Studies index.
 *
 * A4 is the flagship. A1 and A3 are supporting work and are described from what their
 * committed reports and summaries actually found, which in both cases is narrower and
 * more interesting than their titles suggest. A2 has no exhibits in the public profile
 * and is listed as unavailable rather than as a study a reader could reproduce.
 *
 * Stages B, C and D are not studies. They appear once, under a heading that says they are
 * future work, and they carry no result, no figure and no card.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import StatusTable from "../../components/StatusTable.astro";
import { baseScenario } from "../../scripts/scenarios";
import { NAV } from "../../scripts/site";
import { href } from "../../lib/urls";

const base = await baseScenario();

const contents = [
  { id: "flagship", label: "Flagship case" },
  { id: "supporting", label: "Supporting studies" },
  { id: "unavailable", label: "Not available publicly" },
  { id: "status", label: "Status at a glance" },
  { id: "roadmap", label: "Not yet done" },
];

const description =
  "Four synthetic gas material-balance studies: the flagship counterexample A4, the supporting studies A1 and A3, and A2, whose public exhibits are withheld on rights grounds.";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Studies"
  description={description}
  canonicalPath="/studies/"
  nav={NAV}
  contents={contents}
>
  <h1>Studies</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Four studies. One is the flagship; two support it and are worth reading for what they
    found rather than for what their titles promise; one cannot ship its exhibits in the
    public profile because its inputs are someone else's data.
  </p>

  <section id="flagship" aria-labelledby="flagship-h">
    <h2 id="flagship-h">Flagship case</h2>

    <article class="card card--flagship" aria-labelledby="card-a4">
      <p class="eyebrow">A4 — misleading fit counterexample</p>
      <h3 id="card-a4">
        <a href={href("/studies/a4/")}>When a straight p/Z line is convincing and wrong</a>
      </h3>
      <p>
        A volumetric p/Z fit to {base.observations} quarterly noise-free observations of an
        aquifer-supported reservoir returns R-squared = {base.rSquared.toFixed(6)} and a gas
        in place {base.errorPct.toFixed(3)} percent too high, with the bias
        {" "}{Math.abs(base.biasOverStderr).toFixed(1)} times the fit's own standard error.
        The study then attacks its own result: a bitwise reduction to the volumetric limit at
        zero aquifer productivity, a timestep-refinement check, an estimator-free oracle, a
        matched pair of detector controls, and a measured power curve for the detectability
        question.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nine exhibits, a scenario explorer over the eight computed aquifer strengths, a
        decision memo, and a limitations section that includes the study's own retractions.
      </p>
      <p class="card__meta">
        Synthetic. Reproduced byte for byte from a committed snapshot in the canonical
        environment. Pre-registration rests on the author's word and the prospective ordering
        is unverified — the study says so.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section id="supporting" aria-labelledby="supporting-h">
    <h2 id="supporting-h">Supporting studies</h2>

    <p>
      Both were run before A4 and both returned something other than what their briefs
      expected. Each is summarised here from its own committed report.
    </p>

    <article class="card" aria-labelledby="card-a1">
      <p class="eyebrow">A1 — volumetric baseline</p>
      <h3 id="card-a1">
        <a href={href("/studies/a1/")}>The exact recovery, and how narrow "exact" is</a>
      </h3>
      <p>
        On data generated by the volumetric balance with no measurement error, the p/Z
        inverse recovers gas in place with a relative error of exactly zero at constant
        deviation factor and +5.40e-14 with a pressure-dependent one. The interesting part is
        the seeded-defect diagnostic that followed: a whole class of defects — any uniform
        multiplicative error in the ordinate, a wrong standard-volume basis included — leaves
        the recovered inventory <em>exactly unchanged</em>, so the zero is evidence only
        against the defect classes the design can see.
      </p>
      <p>
        The study's own headline is not the zero. It is the −5.74 percent error the same
        estimator produces, at R-squared 0.9971, when the analyst reuses the initial
        deviation factor instead of the pressure-dependent one.
      </p>
      <p class="card__meta">
        Synthetic. Numerical verification only, and the report says that is the only one of
        the four statements it reaches. Post-hoc diagnostics D1 and D2 are not pre-registered
        and not gated.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="card" aria-labelledby="card-a3">
      <p class="eyebrow">A3 — uncertainty experiments</p>
      <h3 id="card-a3">
        <a href={href("/studies/a3/")}>The error budget that re-ranks itself</a>
      </h3>
      <p>
        An ordinate-side measurement error budget on a synthetic closed tank. At the declared
        survey programme, random pressure scatter is the largest single contributor at 1.32
        percent of G and a shared calibration bias is second at 0.86 percent —
        <strong> and that ranking is not robust</strong>. Halving the assumed scatter reverses
        the top two. That reversal was pre-registered as an inconclusive condition, and the
        condition triggered, so the ranking of the top two is reported as a restatement of an
        assumed number rather than as a result.
      </p>
      <p>
        What does survive: the <em>level</em> of the deviation factor is irrelevant to a p/Z
        gas in place to machine precision, while its <em>shape</em> across the observed window
        moves G roughly one for one. A shared offset does not shrink with more surveys and a
        random one does. Supplying psig where psia was required makes the reservoir look
        smaller.
      </p>
      <p class="card__meta">
        Synthetic. Ordinate-only: cumulative production is never perturbed, so the budget
        answers for the pressure side of the line and not the volume side.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section id="unavailable" aria-labelledby="unavailable-h">
    <h2 id="unavailable-h">Not available in the public profile</h2>

    <article class="card card--unavailable" aria-labelledby="card-a2">
      <p class="eyebrow">A2 — independent PVT check</p>
      <h3 id="card-a2">
        <a href={href("/studies/a2/")}>Availability: exhibits withheld on rights grounds</a>
      </h3>
      <p>
        A2 compares the library's deviation-factor and viscosity correlations against
        laboratory-grade reference equations of state. It is the only study in this project
        that compares the code against values it did not produce — and the only one whose
        inputs this repository cannot redistribute.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>No figure, no table and no number from A2 appears anywhere on this site.</strong>
        {" "}Its protocol, report and results are excluded from the public tree along with the
        reference extract they quote, and its run script exits non-zero when the extract is
        absent rather than producing a result from nothing.
        {" "}<a href={href("/studies/a2/")}>What it is, why it is withheld, and what you would
        need to run it.</a>
      </p>
      <p class="card__meta">
        <strong>Not independently reproducible from this repository.</strong> A reader must
        obtain the reference data themselves before any part of A2 can be run.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section id="status" aria-labelledby="status-h">
    <h2 id="status-h">Status at a glance</h2>

    <StatusTable
      caption="What each study ships in the public profile, and where that was established."
      captionTitle="Studies"
      rows={[
        {
          check: "A4 — misleading fit counterexample",
          scope: "Nine exhibits, eight computed aquifer cases, decision memo",
          environment: "CPython 3.13.2 on the author's machine, 2026-09-13",
          state: "PASS",
          note: "re-run into a fresh directory and, in the canonical environment, diffed byte for byte against the committed snapshot",
          evidence: { label: "Case page", path: "/studies/a4/" },
        },
        {
          check: "A1 — volumetric baseline",
          scope: "Numerical verification of the p/Z inverse, plus two post-hoc diagnostics",
          environment: "CPython 3.13.2 on the author's machine, 2026-09-13",
          state: "PASS",
          note: "byte-identical on re-run; D1 and D2 are not pre-registered and not gated",
          evidence: { label: "Case page", path: "/studies/a1/" },
        },
        {
          check: "A3 — uncertainty experiments",
          scope: "Ordinate-side measurement error budget on a synthetic closed tank",
          environment: "CPython 3.13.2 on the author's machine, 2026-09-13",
          state: "PASS",
          note: "byte-identical on re-run; one pre-registered inconclusive condition triggered and is reported as inconclusive",
          evidence: { label: "Case page", path: "/studies/a3/" },
        },
        {
          check: "A2 — independent PVT check",
          scope: "Correlations against laboratory-grade reference equations of state",
          environment: "not run anywhere in the public profile",
          state: "BLOCKED",
          note: "its reference inputs are not redistributed; the run script exits 1 when they are absent",
          evidence: { label: "Availability page", path: "/studies/a2/" },
        },
        {
          check: "Hosted continuous integration",
          scope: "Any check running anywhere other than the author's machine",
          environment: "not run anywhere",
          state: "NOT RUN",
          note: "no hosted run is reported here; every check above ran on the author's own machine",
          evidence: { label: "Methods", path: "/methods/" },
        },
        {
          check: "Human peer review",
          scope: "Review by a person other than the author",
          environment: "not run anywhere",
          state: "NOT RUN",
          note: "every review pass was conducted under a different role by the same author",
          evidence: { label: "About", path: "/about/" },
        },
      ]}
      id="studies-status"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="roadmap" aria-labelledby="roadmap-h">
    <h2 id="roadmap-h">Not yet done</h2>

    <Callout kind="note" title="This is future work, not results">
      <p>
        Nothing in this section has been run. It is listed so that a reader can see what the
        project intends and can tell it apart from what it has actually produced. None of it
        has a figure, a number or a study page, and none of it should be read as a result.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Stage B — pressure-transient analysis.</strong> A written protocol exists in
        the repository under <code>docs/protocols/</code>. No run, no results, no exhibits.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Stage C and Stage D.</strong> Not started, and not scoped beyond a heading.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The abscissa.</strong> A3's own recommendation, and the one gap it says it is
        least able to defend: put a metering bias and a random metering error through the
        existing machinery so that the error budget covers the volume axis as well as the
        pressure axis.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A companion diagnostic case for A4.</strong> Pre-register a test of the
        fitted-versus-measured initial p/Z mismatch and of the Cole plot's power against the
        same noise sweep, with the deviation-factor correlation mismatched between generator
        and analyst, and with an independent seed per noise level.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <style>
    .card {
      border: 1px solid var(--c-rule);
      border-radius: var(--radius-md);
      padding: var(--space-5);
      margin-block: var(--space-5);
      background: var(--c-surface);
    }
    .card--flagship {
      border-width: 2px;
      border-color: var(--c-border-strong);
    }
    .card--unavailable {
      border-style: dashed;
    }
    .card h3 {
      margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    }
    .card__meta {
      font-size: var(--text-sm);
      color: var(--c-ink-2);
      border-block-start: 1px solid var(--c-rule);
      padding-block-start: var(--space-3);
      margin-block-end: 0;
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
